ns atomic number is 6 so it's got  six protons six neutrons we'll need to  know that an elastic collision  that kinetic energy is being conserved  and that means that after the collision  our two masses are not sticking together  what we can do is write down what the  kinetic energy is going to be on both  sides so before and after the collision  and we know it's conserved so we can put  an equal sign in the middle there if you  cancel out the common terms which is 1/2  m in this case you get v1 squared is  equal to v2 squared plus 2 V 3 squared  so I'm rewriting this equation in terms  of V 3 and then I'm recognizing that V 1  squared minus V 2 squared can be  expanded out in the into these two  brackets V 1 minus V 2 and V 1 plus V 2  that's going to help us combine it with  an equation that we'll find soon that  was all force conserving kinetic energy  now the other thing that's being  conserved here is momentum so we can  write out that in V 1 is equal to em V 2  plus 2 MV 3 that's just conservation  there  then again canceling common terms we can  get V 1 is equal to V 2 plus 2 V 3 we've  got two different equations at this  point but three unknowns so I'm going to  do simultaneous equations and try and  sub in what we found from is considering  momentum into what we were dealing with  before so I'm basically just rearranging  the formulas so that I can sub them in  nicely so doing that allows us to cancel  some terms and we end up with V 1 plus V  2 is equal to V 3 but if we go back and  rearrange what we had before V 3 is  nothing but V 1 minus V 2 over 2 so we  can get rid of V 3 entirely and just  have our equations in terms of V 1 and V  2 this is good because being able to  relate V 1 to V 2 will help us find what  we really want to know which is the  fractional loss of energy finding that  fractional loss of energy remember this  is just  for the neutron we will take the  neutrons kinetic energy before subtract  off the neutrons kinetic energy after  and divide it all by the neutrons  kinetic energy before so kinetic energy  1/2 MV squared and actually writing that  out and canceling those common terms of  1/2 M we get V 1 squared minus V 2  squared over V 1 squared giving us what  we want to find now just sub in our  relation between V 1 and V 2 do a little  bit of arithmetic here and we get that  the final answer is 8 over 9 that's  pretty good so now we actually need to  do all that again for a collision with  carbon the only thing that's going to  change that the carbon is now 12 times  the mass of the neutron so we can might  be able to go a bit faster because  they're doing it again in this example  in my diagram I'm assuming that the  neutron and the carbon move off together  in the same direction  I guess v2 could also go backwards the  neutron could have bounced off and been  going in the opposite direction I worked  through the math was that with that as  well and it doesn't matter whether it  bounces off or goes forward because you  end up squaring the velocity and the  final terms really the absolute value of  that velocity is all that matters there  so again looking at kinetic energy first  and we can jump some of the steps that  we were doing on the previous example to  get V 1 squared equals V 2 squared plus  12 V 3 squared this time and just  rearranging it in a similar way so that  we can cancel some terms again  conserving momentum we end up that v1 is  equal to v2 plus 12 v3 rearranged that  sub it into our kinetic energy equation  we end up with V 1 plus V 2 is equal to  V 3 but the 3 is nothing but V 1 minus V  2 over 12 so we can get rid of v3  entirely get an expression that just  relates V 1 and V 2 then our frictional  loss of energy  very similar thing but now we're subbing  in - 13 / 11 V - as being our value of  v1 and this algebra gets a little bit  more tricky but we end up doing some  fractions we get 48 over 169 as our  fraction there now because the question  is asking for this in terms of decimals  which makes it harder we have to work  that out as a decimal and that will be  0.28 to two decimal places so we need to  go and find the other one decimals - and  that would be zero point eight nine so  then we just need to look at our answers  we have zero point eight nine for the  first one point two eight for the second  one and that gives us the third option  as being correct there our second  question is about molecules bouncing off  a wall and what pressure that's going to  exert on the wall so we have the mass of  a hydrogen molecule is three point three  two times ten to the minus 27 kg if 10  to the 23 hydrogen molecules strike per  second a fixed wall of area two  centimeters squared at an angle of 45  degrees to the normal and rebound  elastically with a speed of 10 to the 3  meters per second then the pressure on  the wall is merely and were asked to  find that value so the basic ideas here  are going to be change in momentum and  working out pressure this is a pretty  easy one to remember pressure as force  over area and in terms of like impulse  force well force is going to be the  change in momentum over the change in  time and if we're dealing with constant  mass which I assume these molecules are  then that's going to come down to mass  times change in velocity over a change  in time starting off just considering a  single molecule we can draw a diagram  we're coming in at an angle losing all  of that momentum and then leaving in the  opposite direction with that momentum  again so because we're being asked about  the pressure that's it exerted on the  wall all we really care about here is  the  horizontal component of what's going on  to the horizontal component of the  change in momentum or the change in  velocity to work out that horizontal  component which I'm calling VH we can do  V one which has our initial velocity  times the sine of 45 that's the same as  doing it the one divided by the square  root of two now because we need to work  out the final velocity in the horizontal  direction we're actually going to do two  times that amount because we're losing  at all getting and gaining it all again  so our change in momentum is going to be  two times that value putting that into  our formula for force we get this and  remember that two over the square root  of 2 is just equal to square root two  and the time that this is going on over  is just one second so we've got a 1 on  the bottom there yeah that was for one  molecule and for n molecules times it by  n that gives us our total force on the  wall then define the total pressure on  that wall we want to divide it by our  area which was 2 centimeters squared so  just plug in all of our values here most  of working this out comes to looking at  our like exponents so we have 10 to the  23 a 10 to the 3 that would be 10 to the  26 we've got a minus 27 so we'd have a  total of 10 to the minus 1 on the top  we've got a 10 to the minus 4 on the  bottom minus 1 minus negative 4 we're  going to end up with 10 to the positive  3 as being the exponent on our answer  and then working out the rest of it in  decimals here it gives us our final  answer which corresponds to option  number three on our exam our last  question now number three we've got a  solid sphere of radius R made of a soft  material of bulk modulus K is surrounded  by a liquid in a cylindrical container a  massless piston of area a floats on the  surface of the liquid covering the  entire cross-section of the cylindrical  container  when a mass M is placed on the surface  of the piston to compress the liquid the  fractional decrement in the radius of  the sphere D R over R is what so  essentially we've got a sphere in a  container and it's being squashed so a  bulk modulus which I think is the only  thing you'll need to know to get started  here is that a bulk modulus is the  change in pressure over like the  fractional change in volume so I've  written the formula here and we're going  to want the I guess absolute value of it  we also need to know the volume of the  sphere here so 4/3 PI R cubed now what  we want is D R over R which is relating  to our radius and that little fraction  there it looks like we're going to need  to differentiate to get there because  that little D R is really indicating  that there's some calculus going on some  infinitesimal change so what we can  start with is by differentiating our  equation for the volume and we can do  that with respect to the radius so we  can find DV d R as being 4 PI R squared  notice that that is the surface area of  a sphere so actually that makes sense  now we've kind of got a DV in our  formula for the bulk modulus so if we  have a look at that and take inspiration  from there and do a DV over V we can  cancel out quite a few terms actually  and we can get left with a formula that  has an R and a d R in it now that's  pretty close to what we want so if we  rearrange for that we end up getting DV  over 3v and if we rearrange our formula  for bulk modulus in terms of the change  in volume over volume that would be  equal to the change in pressure over the  modulus itself so we have enough we need  to be able to sub things and so pressure  is force over area our change in  pressure is going to be coming from our  masse that we've been putting on this so  M times G for our force over the area a  of that piston and they've given it to  us that the bulk modulus is K meaning  that all this comes to be mg over three  ka and that is one of our answers so  that's very nice I hope that gave you  some more insight into solving these  problems from my understanding you don't  get calculators in this exam correct me  if I'm wrong but if you don't get  calculators actually that makes some of  those last steps very difficult those  converting fractions to decimals and  working out those final decimal answers  so congrats to anyone who can do that  and good luck to anyone who's preparing  for exams like this thanks for watching  you

